extends ../../layout/blog-layout.pug

block title
    - var date = "2026-08-09"
    - var title = "DIY Smart TV Media Center Dashboard: Fullscreen App Launcher for Debian and Xfce"
    - var description = "Set up a fullscreen kiosk-style dashboard on Debian with Xfce, using a Python/Flask backend to launch apps, URLs, and system actions from a smart TV-like app grid."
    - var thumbnail = "https://www.teklynk.com/assets/images/dashboard_IMG_0951.jpg"
    - var pageUrl = "https://www.teklynk.com/blog/diy-smart-tv-media-center-dashboard-fullscreen-app-launcher-for-debian-and-xfce"
    - var gitHubUrl = "https://github.com/teklynk/smart-dashboard"
    - var skills = []
    | Teklynk - #{title}

block content

    img.img-fluid.mb-4.shadow-sm(src="/assets/images/dashboard_IMG_0951.jpg", alt="Photo of livingroom TV displaying the Smart Dashboard. A row of rectactular buttons and icons for various streaming services, apps and tools. Blueish background photo of a mountain.")

    h2 The Story

    p I used Amazon Fire TV sticks on all my TVs for years. They worked, but the ads, tracking, recommendations, and clutter got old. I only used a handful of apps, while the interface was full of content I didn't need.
    p I also had privacy concerns because Fire TV required an Amazon account. I tried to block as much as possible with Pi-Hole and later used Proton VPN with NetShield.
    p In the end, I wanted a much simpler experience: a UI that showed only the apps I use and a few useful settings.
    p A few years ago, I found five 2011 Mac Minis on eBay during the Raspberry Pi shortage. The older Mac Minis were even cheaper than a Pi at the time.
    p That stack of Mac Minis made me wonder whether they could become basic media-center PCs. They are older Intel i5 machines with Intel graphics and only output 1080p, but that is enough for the use case.
    p I decided to build a Python/Flask web app that starts on boot and presents a custom Smart TV-style UI in fullscreen kiosk mode.
    p I chose Debian with XFCE because it is lightweight and flexible enough for the tweaks I wanted.
    p As a web developer, I used Python for the backend and Flask for the frontend, which let me build the UI using JavaScript, CSS, and JSON configuration files.
    p While researching the idea, I found several Linux desktop apps aimed at media-center PCs, such as Plex HTPC edition, Jellyfin Desktop, Kodi, and VacuumTube. VacuumTube is a desktop clone of the Android/Smart TV YouTube app, and all of these run fullscreen and work with keyboard navigation.
    
    h2 The Concept

    p On boot, a bash script starts the Python/Flask app and opens http://localhost:8080 in a browser with #[code --kiosk]. Running it as a web app lets me assign a #[code --name] and track its process ID. I also set each website to launch as its own web app so it gets its own #[strong .config] directory, stays sandboxed, and behaves more like a desktop application.
    p Native desktop apps such as Plex and VacuumTube launch with commands like #[code flatpak run rocks.shy.VacuumTube --fullscreen]. I chose Flatpak apps because they are easy to install, sandboxed, and consistent across machines.
    p Tools and settings open like desktop apps, with their icons placed in the top-right corner of the UI. These buttons launch things like #[code xfce4-settings-manager], #[code xfce4-session-logout], Bluetooth settings, Network Manager, and whatever else I need.
    p The UI can be navigated with arrow keys or Tab / Shift+Tab, so I can use a wireless PC remote such as the #[a(href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=MX3+Pro+Remote+Control", target="_blank") MX3 Pro, 2.4G Backlit Mini Wireless Keyboard &amp; Infrared Remote].
    p I also wanted to see how far I could strip down XFCE without breaking the system, but first I needed the Python app to launch on boot in a stock Debian/XFCE setup.

    h2 The Process

    p Installing Debian was straightforward. I chose XFCE during setup, enabled SSH, and left other desktop environments unchecked. On the 2011 Mac Minis, I also installed non-free firmware packages for Wi-Fi and Bluetooth so the hardware would work reliably.
    p After install, I added the non-free and non-free-firmware repositories, then installed the browser, Flatpak, and kiosk utilities: #[code xdotool], #[code wmctrl], #[code unclutter], #[code input-remapper], #[code network-manager], #[code bluez], #[code pavucontrol], and other helpers.
    p I installed Brave Origin, enabled Flathub in Flatpak, then installed the dashboard apps I wanted: Plex, Jellyfin Desktop, Kodi, VacuumTube, and a few supporting tools.
    p The Smart Dashboard itself came from the #[strong smart-dashboard] repository. I cloned it, created a Python virtual environment, installed the requirements, and used #[code run.sh] to start the Flask server and launch Brave in kiosk mode.
    p I configured the system so the dashboard starts automatically on login. LightDM auto-logs my user in, and I added #[code run_always.sh] to XFCE Session and Startup so the dashboard opens full screen after each boot.
    p To keep the experience clean, I disabled XFCE panel autostart, turned off screen blanking with #[code xset -dpms s off], and saved a minimal session. I also removed #[code xfce4-desktop] and replaced it with a dummy #[strong xfce4-desktop] executable, so no desktop shows. That keeps the dashboard as the home screen, and closing an app returns me to the same fullscreen interface.
    p I removed the default XFCE keyboard shortcuts, set workspaces to #[strong 1], made HDMI audio the default output, and removed unnecessary startup apps.
    p The #[code server.py] script disables right-click menus for web apps. Since web apps run in kiosk mode, users cannot escape fullscreen.
    p The #[code run_always.sh] script relaunches the dashboard after three seconds if it closes. A confirmation dialog appears when the user tries to close the dashboard with Alt+F4. Either way, the dashboard relaunches and stays available.
    p The dashboard uses JSON files for configuration, so I can define apps, tools, and weather by editing #[strong apps.json], #[strong tools.json], and #[strong weather.json]. Wallpaper images added to #[strong static/backgrounds/] are discovered automatically and randomly rotate with a dissolve effect, giving it a more Smart TV-like feel.

    h2 The End Results

    p The project involved a fair bit of trial and error. I wanted to strip XFCE down as far as possible while still keeping #[strong xfce-settings-manager] and the rest of the XFCE toolset available. I did not want to start from scratch with a barebones window manager like openbox or fluxbox and then rebuild everything manually. It was easier to begin with a working XFCE system and remove what I did not need.
    p The media center now boots quickly and opens the dashboard UI immediately. My setup includes:
    ul
        li #[a(href="https://flathub.org/en/apps/tv.plex.PlexHTPC", target="_blank") PlexHTPC]
        li #[a(href="https://jellyfin.org/", target="_blank") Jellyfin Desktop]
        li #[a(href="https://kodi.tv/", target="_blank") Kodi]
        li #[a(href="https://github.com/shy1132/VacuumTube", target="_blank") VacuumTube]
        li #[a(href="https://twitchmultiview.teklynk.com/", target="_blank") Twitch Multi-Viewer]
        li Brave Search
        li Ring doorbell cam via #[a(href="https://www.scrypted.app/", target="_blank") Scrypted]
        li #[a(href="https://retroassembly.com/", target="_blank") RetroAssembly] from my home server Docker containers
        li #[a(href="https://localsend.org/", target="_blank") LocalSend] for easy file transfers
        li #[a(href="https://flathub.org/en/apps/com.protonvpn.www", target="_blank") ProtonVPN Flatpak] that starts on boot and is automatically minimized with a bash script and #[code xdotool]
        li system tools for settings, logout/shutdown/reboot, network, Bluetooth, and audio
        li XFCE Terminal for quick edits to JSON config files or Flatpak installs

    p The dashboard stays running as the home screen, and closing an app returns me to the same fullscreen interface. The #[code run_always.sh] script relaunches the dashboard if it exits, and Alt+F4 shows a confirmation prompt before it closes.
    p Configuration remains simple: #[strong apps.json], #[strong tools.json], and #[strong weather.json] control the dashboard layout and behavior. Images dropped into #[strong static/backgrounds/] are discovered automatically and rotate with a dissolve effect.
    p The JSON config includes a #[strong fullscreen] option that forces apps fullscreen even if they do not natively support it. This uses #[code wmctrl] to detect the open window and maximize it, and it works with dialogs such as #[code xfce4-settings-manager] and #[code nm-connection-editor].
    p Forcing apps fullscreen means the only way back to the dashboard is to close them, which makes the system feel more like a Smart TV and a single-task interface.
    p I can navigate through the apps with the arrow keys and use Tab to access the tools in the top right.
    p I used #[code input-remapper-gtk] to program an MX3 Pro wireless PC remote. The home button sends #[strong Alt+F4] to close the running app and return to the dashboard. The remote also has an IR-programmable button that I set for my TV volume and power, so I can use one remote for most tasks.
    p I considered adding #[code onboard], a Linux on-screen keyboard. However, the MX3 remote already has a usable keyboard on the back, and most apps like Plex and VacuumTube include their own on-screen keyboard.

    h2 Conclusion

    p I have been running this setup for over a week, and I even built another one for my bedroom TV. It has been stable and useful, and I am still making small tweaks rather than reworking the whole system.
    p The result is a simple, self-hosted media center with no ads, no tracking, and no Amazon sign-in. It only shows the apps I want, and everything is configurable through JSON and CSS.
    p Using Debian and XFCE made it easier to get to a working system first, then remove what I did not need. That saved a lot of time compared to starting from a minimal window manager and rebuilding the environment from scratch.
    p The dashboard feels more like a Smart TV home screen than a traditional desktop. Apps launch fullscreen, the interface stays pinned as the home screen, and a single remote can handle most tasks.
    p If you want a clean living-room interface that is fully under your control, this setup is a good starting point. It can grow over time as you add more apps, tweak the layout, or swap in different web services.

    h3 Here is my current setup as of the writing of this blog post.

